From c18406db95039ea013a643be2b2b32b37e66a00d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Rob Paprocki <hi@rpaprocki.com>
Date: Thu, 1 Oct 2026 08:30:07 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 16/28] linux: build Linux clients under a Linux-safe app name

A Linux build with the app name "Rescue Client (Rob Paprocki)" failed in
build.py: the workflow writes the name into unquoted shell commands
(mkdir -p tmpdeb/usr/share/<name>), the deb Package field, the rpm Name,
systemd unit names and desktop files, and dash stopped at the bracket.
The client itself derives its unit name, process matching, /etc/<name>
paths and link scheme from the lowercased app name at runtime, so a
Linux build cannot keep a display name with spaces either.

Linux builds now get one name everywhere (workflow input and custom.txt
app-name): the app name lowercased, with every run of other characters
turned into a hyphen ("rescue-client-rob-paprocki"). Other platforms and
the label shown in the console keep the full name. A Linux build is
refused with a clear message when that name is shorter than two
characters, starts with a digit (invalid link scheme), contains
"rustdesk" (the workflow's renaming would double it), or is already the
name of a Linux command, package or service (linux_reserved_names.txt):
installing the client would replace /usr/bin/<name> and <name>.service.

The form shows the Linux name as you type, and after a refused build it
keeps the selected platform instead of falling back to Windows.
